  "image_name": "aa12bfb8a2f626829166a589c91bd54b.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several Raman-based spectroscopy approaches and emphasizes how the sample surface strongly affects what can be measured, especially for soft, biological materials. Standard Raman on a flat bulk sample gives a simple, direct measurement, but the signal is weak. In surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy, gold nanoparticles create intense electromagnetic “hot spots” between particles, greatly amplifying Raman signals. For hard materials, this mainly improves detection, including of nearby solutes or contaminants. For soft materials, however, nanoparticles sit on a molecular layer and can interact with it more strongly, leading not only to signal enhancement but also chemical bonding and even photo- or photochemical reactions at the contact points.\n\nThe figure also contrasts tip-enhanced Raman spectroscopy, where a sharp silver or gold scanning probe tip concentrates the field at a tiny region, with shell-isolated nanoparticle-enhanced Raman spectroscopy. In SHINERS, gold nanoparticles coated with a thin silica shell still enhance the Raman response while reducing direct chemical contact with the sample. The biological example with proteins and antibodies suggests this method can probe delicate soft interfaces more gently. Overall, the image shows that Raman enhancement methods increase sensitivity but can also perturb soft matter, so isolation strategies become important.",
